    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d393-a3">

                <pb facs="0732" n="638" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg638"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 3 in No. 67.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Alden to Mr. Porter.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate-General of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi
                                rendition="#italic">Rome</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1887-06-01"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi>
                            1, 1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 143.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    from the Department of State of a telegram in reply to my dispatch No. 139,
                    dated May 4, 1687. In said telegram, I am informed that the granting of the
                    certificate of “<hi rendition="#italic">nulla osta,”</hi> referred to in my
                    dispatch No, 139, is inadmissible.</p>
                <p>Since receiving the telegram I have twice seen the Italian authorities. They
                    inform me that as the Italian law provides that no foreigner can be married in
                    Italy without a certificate of “<hi rendition="#italic">nulla osta,”</hi> and
                    that as the Department of State has forbidden me to issue such certificate, it
                    will henceforth be impossible for any American citizen to be married in Italy,
                    unless the Italian law or the order of the Department should be modified.</p>
                <p>I may add that there is no provision by which my “examination as an expert” could
                    take the place of the usual consular certificates. Thanking the Department for
                    its telegram,</p>
                <p>I am, sir,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Wm. L. Alden.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
